<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">abilities</ansi>

Warriors, rogues, and rangers each have a class ability they use on their
own in a battle, when the moment comes. Nobody types it: like a healer's
heal, it follows from how your company is set up. Who has which:

  a companion   by its archetype, from level 1
  you           by the skill you have trained (level 1 or more)

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The abilities</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">Tackle</ansi>           warriors; for you, the <ansi fg="skill">brawling</ansi> skill
    When: its foe is on its feet and within hand-to-hand reach (not a
    rank deeper with a long weapon), and it has no bow or sling.
    Does: knocks the foe to the ground: 40% when your Speed equals the
    foe's Perception, up to 80% ahead or down to 20% behind, a
    1/{{ config "GamePlay.Combat.StatEdgeSpan" }} of the way per point (<ansi fg="command">help stat-edge</ansi>), and moved the same
    way by its Attack against the foe's Evasion (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>). A knocked-
    down foe loses its next action and is slower for 3 rounds. A tackle
    also breaks a foe's chant (it must start again) or wind-up. Hit or
    miss, the tackle is the warrior's whole turn: no swing that round.
    At most once every 4 combat rounds.

  <ansi fg="command">Opening Strike</ansi>   rogues; for you, the <ansi fg="skill">skulduggery</ansi> skill
    When: its foe is knocked down, stunned, staggered, or exposed, and it
    wields a blade or claws (slashing, stabbing, cleaving), every weapon
    it holds.
    Does: its first blow that lands this round is a critical hit, with
    2 more damage, and 1 more for every 6 levels.
    At most once every 2 combat rounds.

  <ansi fg="command">Aimed Shot</ansi>       rangers; for you, the <ansi fg="skill">track</ansi> skill
    When: it has a bow or sling, and its foe is not already exposed.
    Does: its first shot that lands this round is a critical hit; if it
    gets through the armor, the foe is left exposed (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>),
    an opening for a rogue.
    At most once every 3 combat rounds.

An opening strike or aimed shot replaces one turn's swing, never an
extra one: it still has to hit, can still be blocked, parried, or dodged,
and armor still takes its share (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>). Its critical hit
leaves the weapon's status, like any other.
A member uses an ability only on its own foe in your battle, and only on
a turn it would swing: not while it chants a spell, waits for a slow
weapon, or has lost the action to a status, and nobody uses one while the
company prepares to retreat. The formation rules a swing as usual: a foe
the front row shields is out of an ability's reach. An ability counts as
used when it is tried, even if its foe falls before the blow. Abilities use no
items. Anyone outside your company has none.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Healers and casters</ansi>

Clerics and wizards fight by their spells (<ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>). Two
healers don't heal the same member at once: while one heal is being
chanted on someone, the other healer heals someone else, or fights. A
member's mana reserve keeps a share of its mana back from attack spells,
for heals or the next fight; heals ignore it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Commands</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi>                       everyone's abilities, after their role and rule
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who]</ansi>                 one member's abilities, when each is used
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] abilities off</ansi>   use none in battle (<ansi fg="command">on</ansi> to use them again)
  <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] reserve [percent]</ansi>
                                 keep that much mana back from attack spells
                                 (0 to 90; 0 by default)

Like every strategy, these are set before a battle and kept when you log
out. <ansi fg="command">strategy [who] default</ansi> turns abilities back on and clears the
reserve. In the web client, the Combat tab shows each member's abilities;
click a member to turn them off or on (<ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help brawling</ansi>.

A tackle spends one earned turn, even on a miss. If you earn two turns,
your other turn can be an ordinary attack. No earned turn means no physical
ability that round (<ansi fg="command">help tempo</ansi>).

Warriors see a successful tackle earlier in the same ability pass and avoid
tackling that foe again. A missed tackle still spends its turn.
